WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.787
Many array defects come from ordinary loop logic.

2
00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:05.226
A termination condition uses the wrong comparison.

3
00:00:05.226 --> 00:00:08.362
A loop assumes there is at least one element.

4
00:00:08.362 --> 00:00:12.194
The last valid element is confused with the count of elements.

5
00:00:12.194 --> 00:00:14.982
A size is captured before a container changes.

6
00:00:14.982 --> 00:00:18.466
These are everyday mistakes, which is exactly why patterns matter.

7
00:00:18.466 --> 00:00:21.253
Off-by-one errors usually appear near boundaries.

8
00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:27.176
Zero elements, one element, exactly the maximum allowed size, and just-beyond-limit values deserve explicit attention.

9
00:00:27.176 --> 00:00:35.887
A loop that works for a typical middle-sized collection may still be wrong for an empty range or a range with one valid item.

10
00:00:35.887 --> 00:00:39.371
Range-based loops and standard algorithms reduce unnecessary index handling.

11
00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:44.597
If the code only needs to visit each element, an index may not be needed.

12
00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:52.610
Removing the index removes the need to reason about start value, end value, increment, signedness, and whether the index still belongs to the

13
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:53.307
accessed container.

14
00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:59.579
When indexing is required, check the index before access and keep it tied to the container being accessed.

15
00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:06.199
Avoid deriving an index from one container and applying it to another unless the relationship is explicit and validated.

16
00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:11.773
If a function accepts an index, its contract should state the valid range and failure behavior.

17
00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:14.212
Signed and unsigned interactions deserve careful review.

18
00:01:14.212 --> 00:01:19.787
Container sizes often use unsigned types, while application indexes, deltas, or parsed values may be signed.

19
00:01:19.787 --> 00:01:25.361
A negative value should be rejected before it crosses into an unsigned size or index path.

20
00:01:25.361 --> 00:01:28.497
Clear conversion points make the code easier to trust.

21
00:01:28.497 --> 00:01:31.633
Good iteration code is boring in the best way.

22
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:39.298
It makes the range obvious, handles empty input deliberately, avoids hidden conversion surprises, and uses named helper functions when the range rule

23
00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:41.040
is important enough to repeat.
